\section{Model}{

Let \eqn{\theta} be real, \eqn{\sigma > 0} and \eqn{\nu > 0}. Let \eqn{G}
be a gamma process with
\eqn{G_t - G_s \sim \Gamma((t - s) / \nu, \text{rate } 1 / \nu)}, so that
\eqn{E[G_t] = t} and \eqn{Var(G_t) = \nu t}, and \eqn{B} an independent
standard Brownian motion. The VG process is the Brownian motion with drift
evaluated at the random time \eqn{G_t} given by the gamma process \eqn{G}:
\deqn{X_t = \theta G_t + \sigma B_{G_t}, \qquad t \ge 0.}
It is a Levy process: \eqn{X_0 = 0}, its increments are independent and
stationary, \eqn{E[X_t] = \theta t} and
\eqn{Var(X_t) = (\sigma^2 + \theta^2 \nu)\, t}. The trajectories are not
monotone, purely made of jumps, of finite variation. As \eqn{\nu \to 0}, the
VG process tends to the Wiener process \eqn{\theta t + \sigma B_t}.
}

\section{Exact simulation}{

Random walk approximation, described in \link{DegradSim-package}: each
increment, over a time \eqn{\Delta t_i = t_i - t_{i-1}}, is drawn by one of
two exact methods (argument \code{method}), which give the same distribution,
then the increments are cumulated.

\strong{Method 1, subordination} (\code{method = "subordination"}, default).
Conditionally on the increment \eqn{\Delta G} of the gamma process,
\eqn{X_t - X_s \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta \Delta G, \sigma^2 \Delta G)}:
\enumerate{
\item draw independently \eqn{\Delta G_i \sim \Gamma(\Delta t_i / \nu)} with
scale \eqn{\nu};
\item compute \eqn{\Delta X_i = \theta \Delta G_i + \sigma \sqrt{\Delta G_i}\, Z_i},
with \eqn{Z_i} independent \eqn{\mathcal{N}(0, 1)}.
}

This method follows the definition of the process and also gives the
gamma process \eqn{G}.

\strong{Method 2, difference of two gamma processes} (\code{method = "difference"}).
Let
\deqn{\mu_\pm = \frac{1}{2} \sqrt{\theta^2 + \frac{2 \sigma^2}{\nu}} \pm \frac{\theta}{2} > 0,}
and \eqn{G_+}, \eqn{G_-} two independent gamma processes with shapes
\eqn{t / \nu} and scales \eqn{\nu \mu_\pm}. Then \eqn{X} has the same
distribution as \eqn{G_+ - G_-}: since \eqn{\mu_+ - \mu_- = \theta} and
\eqn{\nu^2 \mu_+ \mu_- = \sigma^2 \nu / 2}, the characteristic function of
\eqn{\Delta G_+ - \Delta G_-} over a time \eqn{\Delta t} is
\deqn{(1 - i u \nu \mu_+)^{-\Delta t / \nu} (1 + i u \nu \mu_-)^{-\Delta t / \nu}
  = \big(1 - i u \theta \nu + \sigma^2 \nu u^2 / 2\big)^{-\Delta t / \nu},}
which is the characteristic function of the VG increment (Madan et al.,
1998).
\enumerate{
\item Draw independently \eqn{\Delta G^+_i \sim \Gamma(\Delta t_i / \nu)} with
scale \eqn{\nu \mu_+} and \eqn{\Delta G^-_i \sim \Gamma(\Delta t_i / \nu)}
with scale \eqn{\nu \mu_-};
\item compute \eqn{\Delta X_i = \Delta G^+_i - \Delta G^-_i}.
}

This method only uses gamma draws.
}

